p.src:
In matrix language we transpose and then complex conjugate: $\begin{pmatrix}
a&b\\
c&d
\end{pmatrix}^{\!\dagger}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
a^*&c^*\\
b^*&d^*
\end{pmatrix}.$ Transposition reflects the entries across the main diagonal; conjugation replaces every $i$ by $-i$. For example, $\begin{pmatrix}
2&6+7i\\
4-i&9+i
\end{pmatrix}^{\!\dagger}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
2&4+i\\
6-7i&9-i
\end{pmatrix}.$ The diagonal entries remain in their positions but are conjugated, while the two off-diagonal entries exchange positions and are conjugated.

